 2: {"ind": 41678, "ctx": "[header] How to avoid using personal language in writing [title] Use the third person point of view. [step] Never use \" i, \" \" my, \" or otherwise refer to yourself in formal academic writing. You should also avoid using the second person point of view, such as by referring to the reader as \" you.", "activity_label": "Education and Communications", "ctx_a": "[header] How to avoid using personal language in writing [title] Use the third person point of view. [step] Never use \" i, \" \" my, \" or otherwise refer to yourself in formal academic writing. You should also avoid using the second person point of view, such as by referring to the reader as \" you.", "ctx_b": "", "split": "val", "split_type": "indomain", "label": 3, "endings": ["\" [substeps] If you are asked a question, try to find something that can be answered by simply ending the discussion with an \" i \" instead of the second person point of view. For example, if a reader asked if you were ethically related, you can answer that question.", "\" in a lot of academic writing, you will add to the title of the essay using third person to show that you are the reader instead of the author. Using this method helps make in-text manuscripts cool and collected, rather than essay paper expressing your thesis, so you can apply this to the entire essay.", "\" [substeps] Always use the third person point of view while using \" i. \" this is the third person point of view used in formal writing, not \" you.", "\" instead, write directly about your subject matter in the third person. [substeps] For example, replace \" i think the most important part of your day is having a good breakfast, \" with \" a nutritious breakfast is an important part of a healthy diet."], "source_id": "wikihow~68605"}
